WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.712 --> 00:00:02.840
STEPHANIE WONG: As your
cloud number grows,

00:00:02.840 --> 00:00:05.900
how do you connect each VPC
without the headache of setting

00:00:05.900 --> 00:00:07.830
up a mountain of VPNs?

00:00:07.830 --> 00:00:10.640
And don't get me started about
the performance overhead.

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.230
Well, there's a
brilliant feature

00:00:12.230 --> 00:00:14.890
I'd like to introduce to you
to save you from all of that--

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:16.040
VPC Peering.

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:16.936
Stay tuned.

00:00:16.936 --> 00:00:20.268
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:23.130 --> 00:00:25.980
As a very hand-wavy
explanation, cloud providers

00:00:25.980 --> 00:00:28.440
build their network with
a series of data centers

00:00:28.440 --> 00:00:31.620
which they position around the
world in strategic locations.

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.870
Since there's already
internet topography,

00:00:33.870 --> 00:00:35.370
they can rely on
the public internet

00:00:35.370 --> 00:00:38.010
to deal with the connections
between these data centers

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.100
without having to do too much
additional lifting, which

00:00:41.100 --> 00:00:44.160
is fine, but introduces a
large problem of security

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.600
for your data.

00:00:45.600 --> 00:00:47.100
Any content from
the public internet

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.340
is vulnerable to all sorts
of behavior, not to mention

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:53.640
the performance issues, which
is why corporations utilize

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:57.570
virtual private networks, VPNs,
and virtual private clouds,

00:00:57.570 --> 00:00:58.950
VPCs.

00:00:58.950 --> 00:01:00.600
These allow customers
and employees

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:03.540
to securely access cloud
resources despite being

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.410
on the public internet.

00:01:05.410 --> 00:01:08.460
Now, as your cloud footprint
grows and your team complexity

00:01:08.460 --> 00:01:10.680
grows, one thing
that starts to happen

00:01:10.680 --> 00:01:13.230
is something called
VPC islanding.

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:16.290
That is, as you start to enable
more data center regions to be

00:01:16.290 --> 00:01:19.260
used by your service, you start
to get more and more complexity

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:22.530
overhead to bootstrap, manage,
and maintain these VPCs in each

00:01:22.530 --> 00:01:23.520
of your regions.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:26.610
Thankfully, Google Cloud has
an answer for this headache.

00:01:26.610 --> 00:01:28.950
By default, all
VPCs on Google Cloud

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.840
are global, meaning
you don't need

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:34.770
to set up a VPC for each
region, which is pretty nice.

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:36.670
You get to build it
once and move on.

00:01:36.670 --> 00:01:38.910
But this doesn't work
for every organization.

00:01:38.910 --> 00:01:40.950
Some structures need
more fine-grained control

00:01:40.950 --> 00:01:43.260
over VPC deployment,
effectively needing

00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:46.320
to redeploy different VPCs
across different regions

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.180
for isolation purposes.

00:01:48.180 --> 00:01:51.390
And for this, we need
to setup VPC peering.

00:01:51.390 --> 00:01:53.880
This is useful in cases where
you have several network

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:55.600
administrative domains.

00:01:55.600 --> 00:01:59.070
For example, you might have a
VPC for the finance department

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:01.510
and another for the
accounting department.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:03.870
The finance department needs
access to all the resources

00:02:03.870 --> 00:02:05.220
that are in their
accounting department,

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:07.230
and the accounting
department needs access

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:09.990
to all the resources in
the finance department.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:13.740
Or imagine that you have two
VPCs connected via VPC peering,

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.360
and you'd like to
reach both of them

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.570
from an on-prem network
with a single VPN.

00:02:18.570 --> 00:02:21.360
This is a very common use
case, as many managed services

00:02:21.360 --> 00:02:25.140
and GCP use VPC peering,
including Cloud SQL.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.500
Behind the scenes, VPC peering
uses our global virtual network

00:02:28.500 --> 00:02:30.900
backbone to create a
connection by allowing networks

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:32.490
to exchange routes.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:34.440
It isn't a gateway
or a VPN connection

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:36.690
and doesn't rely on a
separate piece of hardware

00:02:36.690 --> 00:02:39.240
so that there's no single
point of failure or a bandwidth

00:02:39.240 --> 00:02:40.440
bottleneck.

00:02:40.440 --> 00:02:42.510
Let's walk through
setting up VPC peering.

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:44.370
What we're doing today
is creating a VPC

00:02:44.370 --> 00:02:46.860
peering so a WordPress
deployment in one network

00:02:46.860 --> 00:02:49.440
can communicate privately
with a MySQL deployment

00:02:49.440 --> 00:02:51.060
in another network.

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:55.020
In this project, I have
two VPCs, VPC 1 and VPC 2,

00:02:55.020 --> 00:02:57.330
with non-overlapping
IP ranges, and I want

00:02:57.330 --> 00:02:59.280
to set up peering between them.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.880
I've also deployed a WordPress
and MySQL instance in each VPC,

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:04.860
respectively.

00:03:04.860 --> 00:03:07.860
To create a peering connection
between the two VPCs,

00:03:07.860 --> 00:03:11.520
head to the VPC peering page
and click the Create Connection

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:12.810
button.

00:03:12.810 --> 00:03:15.570
Click Continue,
then enter a name

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:17.670
for the peering connection,
and select the source

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:19.590
and destination networks.

00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:23.080
This one is from VPC 1 to VPC 2.

00:03:23.080 --> 00:03:25.180
While that's creating,
repeat the above process,

00:03:25.180 --> 00:03:27.230
and create a second
peering connection,

00:03:27.230 --> 00:03:29.410
this time reversing the
source and destination

00:03:29.410 --> 00:03:32.990
networks, VPC 2 to VPC 1.

00:03:32.990 --> 00:03:35.155
Once created, the two
networks will automatically

00:03:35.155 --> 00:03:36.530
connect to each
other, and you'll

00:03:36.530 --> 00:03:39.535
see a green check mark
confirming peering is active.

00:03:39.535 --> 00:03:40.910
Over on the routing
page, you can

00:03:40.910 --> 00:03:43.550
see it's automatically
created routes between the two

00:03:43.550 --> 00:03:46.670
networks, and we now have
a full mesh topology.

00:03:46.670 --> 00:03:48.740
Once the networks are
connected, the next step

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:52.010
is to configure the firewall
to enable traffic between them.

00:03:52.010 --> 00:03:53.660
For security reasons,
you should only

00:03:53.660 --> 00:03:55.370
allow access to
the specific ports

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.450
and from trusted
source IP addresses.

00:03:58.450 --> 00:03:59.950
I've already created
a firewall rule

00:03:59.950 --> 00:04:02.530
that allows TCP access
from the WordPress network

00:04:02.530 --> 00:04:06.840
to the MySQL instance
on port 3306.

00:04:06.840 --> 00:04:09.840
The next step is to ensure that
the deployments in the two VPCs

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:12.180
are able to communicate
with each other.

00:04:12.180 --> 00:04:13.830
At the time of
peering, GCP checks

00:04:13.830 --> 00:04:16.110
to see if there are any
subnets with overlapping IP

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:18.029
ranges between the
two networks or any

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:19.410
of their peered networks.

00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.940
If there is overlap,
there's routing issues,

00:04:21.940 --> 00:04:24.272
and peering can't
be established.

00:04:24.272 --> 00:04:25.980
I'll try to connect
to the MySQL instance

00:04:25.980 --> 00:04:27.520
from the WordPress instance.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:31.200
Here, I've connected to the
WordPress instance using SSH.

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:34.200
After entering my route username
and password and the private IP

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.330
of the WordPress instance,
I can run the command

00:04:36.330 --> 00:04:39.600
to view all available databases
and confirm that the connection

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.030
is successful.

00:04:41.030 --> 00:04:41.700
And that's it.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:44.020
We can confirm VPC
peering is established,

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:46.260
and the two instances
in the different VPCs

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.390
are communicating
over private IPs.

00:04:48.390 --> 00:04:50.220
As you can see, VPC
peering gives you

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.710
security, performance,
and manageability.

00:04:52.710 --> 00:04:55.560
To learn more, check out the
documentation linked below,

00:04:55.560 --> 00:04:58.410
stay tuned for the next
episode, and remember,

00:04:58.410 --> 00:05:01.830
optimizing your network means
freeing up your bandwidth.

00:05:01.830 --> 00:05:05.180
[MUSIC PLAYING]

